# Lockup detection — make B-39's wedge say something before it dies.
#
# B-39 records ~9 hard wedges whose whole description was "the machine goes
# away instantly and netconsole captures nothing at the moment of death". That
# was true, and it was true partly because this kernel had no lockup detector
# of any kind: SOFTLOCKUP_DETECTOR, HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR, DETECT_HUNG_TASK and
# WQ_WATCHDOG were all unset, so nothing was watching. The only thing that ever
# noticed was RCU's stall detector, which is on by default and which finally
# printed something on 2026-08-21:
#
#   rcu: INFO: rcu_preempt detected stalls on CPUs/tasks:
#   rcu:  1-...!: (1 GPs behind) ...  2-...!  7-...!
#   rcu:  (detected by 4, t=5256 jiffies, g=26217, q=324 ncpus=8)
#   Task dump for CPU 1: task:ls state:R running task
#
# Three CPUs stopped making progress and RCU noticed 21 seconds later, by which
# point the hardware watchdog was already most of the way to reclaiming the
# machine. That is the only evidence the project has ever had about this
# failure, and it arrived by luck rather than by instrument.
#
# What each of these buys, on this specific problem:
#
# SOFTLOCKUP_DETECTOR — a per-CPU hrtimer/watchdog thread pair that fires when
#   a CPU spends 20 s in the kernel without scheduling. Faster than RCU's 21 s
#   stall timeout in practice and, unlike RCU, it names the CPU that is stuck
#   rather than the CPUs that failed to report.
# HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR (buddy) — arm64 here has no PMU NMI, so the buddy
#   detector is the only option: each CPU checks its neighbour's timer counter.
#   It catches the case the soft detector cannot, a CPU wedged with interrupts
#   disabled, which is exactly what "netconsole captured nothing" looks like.
# DETECT_HUNG_TASK — for the other shape, where nothing spins but a task sits
#   in D state behind a lock or an unfinished DMA for 120 s.
# WQ_WATCHDOG — workqueue stalls. mediatek-drm, panfrost and the USB stack all
#   do real work from workqueues; a stalled one is otherwise silent.
# RCU_CPU_STALL_CPUTIME — adds how much CPU time the stalled CPUs actually
#   consumed, which distinguishes "spinning" from "stopped dead".
#
# Deliberately NOT panicking on any of them (BOOTPARAM_*_PANIC left unset).
# A panic would stop the log at the first event; here the machine is already
# going to be reclaimed by the hardware watchdog either way, and the useful
# thing is as many netconsole lines as possible on the way down, from as many
# CPUs as possible. Pair with `sysctl kernel.softlockup_all_cpu_backtrace=1`
# and `kernel.hardlockup_all_cpu_backtrace=1` after each boot.
#
# Cost: a per-CPU hrtimer and a watchdog thread. This is what every distro
# kernel ships; it is not a debug build.
CONFIG_SOFTLOCKUP_DETECTOR=y
CONFIG_HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR=y
# HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR_PREFER_BUDDY is deliberately absent: it only exists as a
# tie-break when a platform offers BOTH the perf and buddy detectors. Here
# HAVE_HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR_PERF is unset (no PMU NMI on this arm64), so the
# symbol is never offered and asking for it fails gemini-kbuild's merge
# assertion. HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR_BUDDY is selected on its own.
CONFIG_HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR_BUDDY=y
CONFIG_DETECT_HUNG_TASK=y
CONFIG_DEFAULT_HUNG_TASK_TIMEOUT=120
CONFIG_WQ_WATCHDOG=y
CONFIG_RCU_CPU_STALL_CPUTIME=y
